Bug: a CU is built again for a header that its own build found

Since cda0d9b3 a file the removal has not seen before has changed. A header that
only a parse finds, here p.h through <p.h>, which the scan does not follow, is first
seen by the removal in the request after the build that read it. It then counts as
changed, is noticed, and the CU that was just built is out of date again: the trace
says "'p.h' changed, removing its references" and "'a.c' is out of date: 'p.h'
changed" for the second push. Measured on ffmpeg (WSL, session d25255b8,
2026-11-18): 93 more files out of date after the first build round, 2.6s before the
next round starts, and -getproject 0.5s slower, since on a cold start every file is
first seen and each gets a walk over the reference table. The binary from e3a620d0
passes this test.

The likely fix: the parse records the seen token of each file it reads, as the scan
does with startScanOf(), so its references belong to the content they came from.
Then a file the removal sees for the first time has had no parse and no references,
and the old first-sight branch (record the token, remove nothing) can come back.
test_a_header_first_seen_after_a_parse_found_it_has_changed must stay green.
